0 stated in the suitability report. The cashflow models show the Aviva Platform Personal Pension opening value as £82,692, consistent with the post-contribution figure. The expenditure modelled in the cashflow (implied from income/expenditure charts) is stated at approximately £2,206/month in the report, but the fact find records essential expenditure of £2,493/month plus £800 discretionary plus ~£5,000 annually on holidays. The research notes record 'Cash Reserve: £100k+' and objectives centred on tax-efficient investment of the inheritance and reviewing the existing pension, consistent with the report's objectives 1–4. No evidence in the file explicitly records a target retirement income of £2,200 net per month as a client-stated figure independent of the report itself.","discrepancies":["Retirement age conflict: the suitability report records the client's objective as being 'fully retired by age 60' (per [P18] objective 3), but the cashflow models ([P14], [P15]) set Anthony's 'Retire' event at age 67 in 2037 — a 7-year discrepancy. The report does not resolve or acknowledge this.","Cashflow modeller expenditure input (~£2,206/month) is materially lower than the stated current expenditure of ~£3,710/month equivalent (£2,493 essential + £800 discretionary + ~£417/month holiday equivalent) from [P3]. The lower figure is used to demonstrate no shortfall, but may understate the income requirement.","GIA investment amount discrepancy: the suitability report recommends a GIA investment of £57,120 but the cashflow model [P11] records the Aviva GIA opening value as £52,326 — a difference of £4,794. The report also states the client's intention was to invest 'remaining £60,000' of inheritance, which neither figure matches exactly.","The target retirement income of approximately £2,200 net per month is stated in the report as an objective, but the cashflow modeller input uses £2,206/month as current expenditure, conflating expenditure with income target. Current actual expenditure is substantially higher at ~£3,710/month.","No priority ranking is given for the objectives, yet multiple goals compete for the same £100,000 inheritance capital (renovations £40,000, GIA £57,120, pension contribution £2,880, ISA transfer from existing savings) — the report does not explicitly resolve the allocation or acknowledge the competition.","The report states the client recently retired and has stopped working altogether, yet simultaneously asserts earnings are sufficient to support the £3,600 gross pension contribution, which is internally inconsistent with the retirement status."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The most material conflict for this check is the retirement age discrepancy. The research notes [P18] clearly record the client's objective as being 'fully retired by age 60', which would imply a planning horizon to age 60 (approximately 5 years from advice date at age 55). However, the cashflow models in both the current situation and stress-test scenarios ([P14], [P15]) set Anthony's retirement event at age 67 — the state pension age — not age 60. The suitability report does not address this discrepancy, explain why the planning horizon has shifted to age 67, or demonstrate that the recommended investments and pension strategy are consistent with a retirement at age 60. This is a structural conflict: the client states a goal (retire at 60) that the planning model does not reflect (models to 67). The client is already described as having retired from his factory role and stopped part-time work, so in one reading he has already retired — but the cashflow treats age 67 as the retirement event. The report does not reconcile these. The expenditure modelling discrepancy is also material: the cashflow uses ~£2,206/month to show no shortfall, but actual stated expenditure is approximately £3,710/month. Using the lower figure flatters the cashflow outcome and may mask a genuine income shortfall against the client's actual