her a pension contribution is possible. P16 records: 'Retired, gets DB income, but works part-time. When will he stop? Defo 60?' The cashflow modelling scenarios (P6, P8, P9, P11, P14) evidence planning beyond the retirement age in a structured way. The risk profile report (P18, P20) records no additional notes on investment target or agreed risk level discussion. No file evidence specifically records the administration-simplification objective, the 'do not move Chartered savings' objective, or the IHT monitoring objective as client-stated goals.","discrepancies":["The report asserts an objective to simplify administration by consolidating onto one Aviva Platform (objective 4); no fact find note, file note or needs analysis records this as a client-stated objective.","The report asserts an objective not to move the Chartered savings account at this time (objective 7); no fact find note or file note records this as a client-stated goal — it is presented as client-stated in the report.","The report asserts an IHT monitoring objective (objective 8) and marks it as not client-stated; no supporting evidence in the file records this need having been identified or discussed with the client.","The report states the target retirement income as approximately £2,200 net per month and the cashflow modeller uses approximately £2,206 per month as current expenditure — these are used interchangeably; the stated current expenditure in the report is approximately £3,293–£3,710 per month (£2,493 essential + £800 discretionary + £5,000 annual holidays), which is materially inconsistent with the £2,200/£2,206 modelled figure and raises a concern about whether the income target is evidence-based or artificially low.","The fact find notes (P3) record the Cash ISA transfer as contingent ('would have to be really worth his while — not dead set on doing this') and P15 states 'not likely though', yet the report presents it as a positive client objective.","The report does not state a priority ranking for the eight objectives; the fact find declaration (P2) refers to objectives 'identified and prioritised in this document' but no ranking is recorded in either the report or the fact find.","The retirement target age of 60 is evidenced in P3 and P16 but the report does not explicitly state this as a planning horizon anchor in the objectives section (though it underlies the cashflow modelling).","The risk profile research report (P18, P20) records no notes on the investment target discussion or agreed risk level rationale, leaving the risk-alignment objective (objective 1) without evidenced discussion notes."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"Most core objectives are supported by evidence. The £2,200 net per month income target in retirement is recorded in P3 ('Min income in retirement is £2,200 net per month') and corroborated by P15, satisfying the minimum corroboration requirement for that objective. The pension review objective is evidenced in P3 ('Review his Aviva pension and look to improve upon it'). The tax-efficient investment of the inheritance is evidenced in P3 and P15. The retirement age of 60 is evidenced in P3 and P16. The pension contribution objective is evidenced in P15 (earnings and contribution assessment noted). The risk-alignment objective is partially supported by the agreed risk level of 4 in the research report (P10), though P18 and P20 record no discussion notes. However, several concerns arise. First, the administration-simplification objective (4) and the 'do not move Chartered savings' objective (7) appear in the report as client-stated but are not recorded in any fact find, file note or needs analysis in the file — their evidential basis is absent. Second, the IHT monitoring objective (8) is adviser-identified (correctly not client-stated) but no file evidence records this need being identified or discussed. Third, the income target of £2,200/month conflicts materially with stated current expenditure of approximately £3,293–£3,710/month; the cashflow modeller uses £2,206/month as the expenditure input, which is not reconciled against the higher current expenditure figure. This raises a genuine concern about whether the income target is grounded in a properly evidenced needs analysis or whether the cashflow model is understating future income needs. Fourth, the Cash ISA transfer objective is presented more positively in the report than the fact find and file notes justify — both P3 and P15 record the client as uncertain and unenthusiastic. Fifth, no priority order is recorded anywhere despite the fact find declaration referencing prioritised objectives. The vulnerability overlay: no vulnerability indicators are present in the file that materially affect this check. The guard that advisers paraphrase explains the wording differences between the report and file notes for the core objectives, but does not explain the absence of any file evidence for objectives 4, 7 and 8, nor the income target inconsistency.","citations":[{"passageId":"P3","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"Review his Aviva pension and look to improve upon it. Client will complete ATRQ in his own time. Wants to be fully retired by 60. Min income in retirement is £2,200 net per month. Also looking at investment advice on £60k of his Dad's property money coming in this month, and the £40k cash he currently has so as to avoid paying tax on interest. He has already used his full ISA allowance this year, so has his wife. Potentially interested in transferring his cash ISA to S&S ISA but would have to be really worth his while - not dead set on doing this.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Notes - Barnes.md","category":"C","quote":"About to receive circa £100k but will use £40k on kitchen/bathroom.\nHas another £40k in high interest savings, but only look at investing the £60k. Possibly move ISAs – not likely though. Only ATR 4.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P16","source":"Notes -