It is your responsibility to ensure there is sufficient cash in your cash account to pay any charges. If there's insufficient cash, we will automatically sell enough investments to pay any charges. For further information please see 'What are the charges for the Investment Portfolio?' on page 4.
Where you're taking regular withdrawals, your adviser can choose on your behalf how the withdrawal is funded. This could be either from particular assets your adviser has specified, or equally or pro rata across the assets. However, if the withdrawal method chosen by your adviser doesn't generate enough cash to pay your withdrawal, we'll disinvest proportionately across the assets in order to do so. Any withdrawal will reduce the value of your investments. We won't automatically disinvest if you're only invested in funds which are in deferment or suspension, structured products and exchange-traded instruments. If you are only partially invested in funds which are in deferment or suspension, structured products and exchange-traded instruments, then we'll disinvest from any other investments you hold.
The price of some investments includes initial and yearly costs that will have an effect on your investments. Initial costs will have an immediate effect on the value of those investments.
How funds take their charges has an impact on your investment growth or income. If charges are taken from your investment, its growth may be constrained. If charges are taken from your income, then that will be eroded.
From time to time, we may contact you and ask you to make decisions about your investments (for example, corporate actions). If you don't get back to us within the timescales we give you, or there's insufficient cash in your cash account, we may be unable to act upon your instructions.
