m and investment research was conducted across all four recommended wrappers, and the pension switch research [P4] corroborates the cost reduction rationale. The ISA research [P3]/[P6] corroborates the Bed and ISA rationale and the whole-of-market selection. The fund performance comparison in [P14] corroborates the risk-realignment and volatility rationale for the pension fund switch.\n\nHowever, several material weaknesses arise:\n\n1. Alternatives: The policy research file [P8] has entirely blank fields for 'Alternative Strategies' across all schemes. While the report references the existing Vanguard funds as an alternative considered and rejected (with performance data), no documented alternatives are recorded for the GIA, ISA wrapper choice, or pension provider selection. The ISA research shows only 1 product from 168 matched filters — the filter set was so narrow it produced a single result, which means no genuine comparison was made and documented. This is a missing component for alternatives.\n\n2. GIA amount discrepancy: The £57,120 GIA investment versus the stated £60,000 intention is not reconciled in the report. The £2,880 difference matching the net pension contribution strongly implies deliberate structuring, but the report does not say this. This is a weak justification — a client reading the report cannot follow the arithmetic.\n\n3. Cashflow modelling inconsistency: The £2,206/month modelled expenditure versus ~£3,710/month stated current expenditure is a material inconsistency that undermines the retirement income sufficiency narrative (OBJ3). The report links recommendations to a retirement income target of ~£2,200/month but the stated actual expenditure is substantially higher. This means the cashflow rationale for whether the recommended strategy meets the client's income objective is built on a figure that does not match the client's stated expenditure — a data inconsistency that weakens the justification.\n\n4. Pension contribution rationale internal inconsistency: The report simultaneously states the client has stopped working and that earnings are sufficient. This is internally inconsistent. The recommended contribution (£2,880 net / £3,600 gross) is the maximum non-earner contribution — suggesting the report is relying on the non-earner allowance rather than actual earnings — but this is not clearly stated, leaving the contribution basis ambiguous.\n\n5. Risk level inconsistency: Using both '6' and '5 and 7' to describe the same existing holdings in different sections is minor but creates potential confusion about the degree of misalignment with risk profile 4.\n\n6. No quantified income shortfall: The report does not quantify the gap between projected retirement income from all sources and the £2,200/month target, so the reader cannot assess whether the recommended strategy is sufficient to meet OBJ3.\n\nThe vulnerability overlay: no vulnerability drivers are recorded in the evidence as materially affecting the suitability assessment for this check.\n\nThe false-positive guards: the two generic rationale items (isClientSpecific: false) are already flagged; the report's own extraction acknowledges them. The charge comparison rationale ('lower costs') is directionally accurate — the platform charge does reduce from 0.32% to 0.21%, though total ongoing charges increase from 0.54% to 0.62% including the new fund — this is disclosed in the switch charges table but the cost reduction rationale could be misleading as framed (it refers only to the platform charge, not the total). This is noted as a potential concern but is primarily addressed under CHK-009.\n\nOn balance: the report has rationale, but it contains material weaknesses — an undocumented alternatives process, an unreconciled GIA figure, a cashflow modelling inconsistency material to the retirement income objective, and an internally contradictory contribution basis. These are sufficient for a Potential Concern at Moderate severity: rationale is present but contains gaps and inconsistencies that a reviewer cannot resolve from the document alone.","citations":[{"passageId":"P8","source":"A Barnes - Policy Research - 2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Alternative Strategies",""]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"A Barnes - Policy Research - 2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Investment Choice Goals",""]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"A Barnes - Policy Research - 2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Income/ Expenditure Goal and Retirement",""]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Investment ISA research report 03-12-2025 11-52.md","category":"F","quote":"1 products from a total of 168 matched the filters applied.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Pension switch report 16-12-2025 10-06.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Valuation:","£79,092.32"]},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Pension switch report 16-12-2025 10-06.md","category":"G","quote":"The charge is 0.32% of the fund value at the point of applying the charge.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"This shows that the recommended Aviva Multi-asset Plus Fund II provides more consistent performance aligned with your Risk Level 4 profile. The fund demonstrates lower volatility than your current mix of Vanguard LifeStrategy 60% and 80% equity funds, which have a combined risk level of 6.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Risk profile report 11 08 2025 08 58.md","category":"D","quote":null,"cells":["No additional notes were recorded"]}],"issueCategories":["Weak justification","Missing component","Data inconsistency"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

