y report's own current situation section. The fact find [P1] records £2,206.67 as the 'Calculated Total Monthly Household Expenditure', which is the figure the cashflow model uses. However, the cashflow files [P20, P9, P12, P14] show a first-year total regular expenditure of £29,915/year (equivalent to ~£2,493/month before Tax & NI, or ~£2,493/month of actual spending items), which is materially higher than £2,206/month. The £2,206/month figure appears to be net of tax (Tax & NI of £3,435/year = ~£286/month is added separately in the cashflow files bringing total to ~£29,915/year = ~£2,493/month of outgoings). This is an internal inconsistency already flagged in the extraction: the cashflow assumption section uses £2,206/month as the expenditure input, but the detailed expenditure statement in the model itself totals £29,915/year gross including tax. The report conflates the net-of-tax expenditure figure (£2,206/month) with total expenditure, underdisclosing the true total. Furthermore, the suitability report's current situation section describes £2,493 essential + £800 discretionary per month (~£3,293/month, ~£39,516/year before holidays), materially exceeding even the £29,915/year model figure. This means the cashflow model appears to exclude discretionary expenditure of ~£800/month that the report itself acknowledges the clients currently incur. The surplus income assumption of 0% saved means all surplus is spent — but if actual expenditure is higher than modelled, the projected surpluses are overstated and the no-shortfall conclusion is at risk of being based on an understated expenditure assumption. The earnings sufficiency claim for the pension contribution is also a genuine concern: the report simultaneously states the client stopped working altogether and that earnings are sufficient, without quantifying those earnings. The £3,600 gross/£2,880 net contribution sits at the maximum non-earner limit, suggesting the report may be relying on the non-earner allowance rather than actual earnings — but this is not stated explicitly. The GIA £57,120 vs £60,000 intention difference is minor and plausibly explained by the net pension contribution of £2,880 being deducted, but this is not stated in the report. The emergency fund of £10,000 matches the fact find exactly [P1] and appears to be a separately identified pot; post-recommendation liquid assets (~£70,000 residual from £190,000 after £120,000 deployments) appear adequate for emergencies, though this reconciliation is not explicitly performed in the report. The total assets figure of £669,092 cannot be corroborated from the supporting documents as retrieved — the cashflow files show opening liquid assets + pension of £225,018, with the balance (~£444,000) presumably attributable to property — but no property valuation appears in the retrieved passages. The life expectancy minor discrepancies (84 vs nearest 85; 87 vs nearest 88 in chart labels) are likely chart label artefacts and not material given the guard about chart parseability. The most significant finding is the expenditure inconsistency: the cashflow model uses £2,206/month which is materially lower than the £2,493 + £800 + holidays figure described in the same report, and the no-shortfall conclusion rests on this understated expenditure input. This is a Data inconsistency and Weak justification concern of Moderate-to-High severity given that the cashflow model's conclusions drive the affordability assessment. The earnings sufficiency claim without quantification is a further Weak justification / Missing evidence concern. Vulnerability: no specific vulnerability factors have been identified in the retrieved passages as materially affecting this affordability assessment.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Calculated Total Monthly Household Expenditure","£ 2,206.67"]},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Total