ge) to 5.9% (high growth, with adviser charge). Reduction in yield rises from 0.6%/0.7% (existing) to 0.7%/0.7% (new). The switch report records no benefits lost on switching ('No benefits were listed for the existing products') [P11]. The GIA illustration [P13] shows projected values declining in real terms in the 'after all charges' column relative to before-charges projections, demonstrating loss scenarios. The cashflow market-crash scenario [P14] is present in the file. The switch report records an initial adviser charge of £0.00 [P11]. The suitability report risk warnings in [P3] cover capital loss and switching risk. Tax sections in [P1][P2] cover GIA, ISA and pension taxation. The capacity for loss and knowledge/experience sections are in [P6]. No vulnerability assessment outcome is recorded in any document.","discrepancies":["The pension switch report records that no benefits are lost on switching ('No benefits were listed for the existing products') [P11], but the suitability report does not explicitly state this — it is silent on whether any guarantees, safeguarded benefits or other protections attached to the existing Aviva plan are foregone. The switch report's finding that no benefits are lost is present only in a supporting document, not surfaced in the body of the suitability report.","The switch report shows a critical yield of 5.9% at the high growth rate scenario [P9][P11], meaning the new solution must grow at 5.9% to match the existing pension at that growth rate. This figure is not referenced in the suitability report body.","The switch report shows an effect of switching of minus £100 (0.1% decrease) at mid and high growth rates [P9][P11]. The suitability report acknowledges higher charges (+0.08%, +£63 p.a.) but does not translate this into a projected capital loss figure for the client.","The GIA is subject to capital gains tax above the £3,000 annual exemption, with rates of 18% (basic rate) and 24% (higher/additional rate) per [P1]. The report discloses CGT rates in a generic tax section but does not apply these specifically to the client's GIA investment of £57,120 or model a scenario where gains are realised.","The report states 'your current earnings are sufficient to support the recommended contribution level' while also stating the client has stopped working — an internal tension noted in the extraction. This uncertainty about the contribution basis is not disclosed as a risk or limitation to the client in the disadvantages/risks section.","No vulnerability assessment outcome is recorded anywhere in the file; the extraction flags this as absent. The report does not consider whether any vulnerability characteristics affect the presentation or comprehension of risk disclosures.","The report does not quantify or illustrate a specific downside/loss scenario for the ISA or GIA investments (e.g. what the portfolio would be worth in a market downturn), beyond the general 'can fall as well as rise' statement and the cashflow market-crash chart."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report has a dedicated switching risk section in the main body, not confined to an appendix, which satisfies the prominence requirement for the pension switch risks. Generic investment risk warnings (capital loss, no guarantee of outperformance) are present [P3]. Tax consequences for the GIA, ISA and pension are addressed in substantive named sections [P1][P2]. The cancellation right and death benefit taxation are disclosed [P5]. Cashflow modelling including a market-crash scenario is referenced [P14][P8]. On the face of it, the report covers the principal categories of risk.\n\nHowever, several gaps remain material:\n\n1. Critical yield and projected capital loss from switching: The switch report quantifies the effect of switching as a £100 (0.1%) decrease in projected fund value at mid and high growth rates [P9][P11], and records a critical yield of 5.9% at the high growth rate [P9][P11]. The suitability report states the new plan costs £63 p.a. more but does not translate this into a projected capital shortfall figure or reference the critical yield. For a pension switch, the critical yield and projected capital impact are material consumer harm figures that a client would need to weigh the recommendation. Their absence from the report body — while present in the switch tool output — is a disclosure shortfall.\n\n2. Benefits lost on switching: The switch report records 'No benefits were listed for the existing products' [P11]. This is a finding that could reassure the client. Its absence from the suitability report means the client is not explicitly told whether any guarantees or protections are being surrendered.\n\n3. GIA tax — client-specific application: The report discloses CGT rates generically [P1] but does not apply them to the client's specific £57,120 GIA holding, where gains above £3,000 will be subject to 18%/24% CGT. Given the client has little investment experience [P6] and the GIA is a new product type for them, a client-specific illustration of the tax consequence would be proportionate.\n\n4. Contribution basis risk: The report acknowledges uncertainty about the client's earnings yet simultaneously asserts sufficiency. This risk — that the £3,600 gross pension contribution may exceed the client's relevant UK earnings and therefore be invalid — is not disclosed as a limitation in the risk section.\n\n5. Vulnerability: No formal vulnerability assessment outcome is recorded. The extraction flags this as absent from the document. The client's stated low investment knowledge [P6] (no understanding of unit trusts, stocks and shares) is recorded but the report does not address whether this affected how risks were communicated or whether any adjustments to the presentation were made. Under the vulnerability overlay, this is noted but assessed as a secondary concern to CHK-010.\n\nThe most material issues are (1) the absence of the critical yield and capital impact figures from the switch in the suitability report body, and (2) the absence of a clear statement that no benefits are lost. These are not explained by any guard: the switch report data exists in the file and the suitability report simply does not surface it. The remaining gaps (GIA tax application, contribution basis risk) are moderate rather than high in isolation. Overall this is a Potential Concern at Moderate severity — the core risk structure is present, but quantified loss data from the switch tool and explicit confirmation of benefits lost/not lost are absent from the report.","citations":[{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"You should remember that unit prices and the value of your pension fund can fall as well as rise and there is no guarantee you will receive a return of your original capital, especially in the early years or if you choose to take an income from it. Past performance is no guarantee of future returns.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"A switch between pension providers does not guarantee a greater pension fund at retirement and there is no guarantee that the new provider's funds will outperform those of your existing provider.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Pension switch report 16-12-2025 10-06.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Critical yield [2]","–","5.9%"]},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Pension switch report 16-12-2025 10-06.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Effect of switching [3]","–","£100 or 0.1% decrease","High growth rate – (5.88%)"]},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Pension switch report 16-12-2025 10-06.md","category":"G","quote":"No benefits were listed for the existing products","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Pension switch report 16-12-2025 10-06.md","category":"G","quote":"Initial adviser charge of £0.00 which is taken from the new solution, reducing the initial inv